::ALG2_U05_L07_Q01::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L07_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.3; MLA.A2.FN.11; MLA.A2.FN.12
Question:
If f(x)=x+6 and g(x)=2x, solve f(x)=g(x).
{
=A. x=6#Correct. x+6=2x gives x=6.
~B. x=3#This divides too early.
~C. x=-6#The sign is wrong.
~D. x=12#This doubles the solution.
}


::ALG2_U05_L07_Q02::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L07_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.3; MLA.A2.FN.11; MLA.A2.FN.12
Question:
If f(x)=x^2 and g(x)=9, solve f(x)=g(x).
{
~A. x=9 only#This forgets the negative square root.
=B. x=-3 and x=3#Correct. x^2=9 gives both the positive and negative square roots.
~C. x=3 only#This misses x=-3.
~D. x=0 and x=9#These do not both satisfy the equation.
}


::ALG2_U05_L07_Q03::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L07_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.3; MLA.A2.FN.11; MLA.A2.FN.12
Question:
What is the y-intercept of f(x)=4x-7?
{
~A. 4#This is the slope.
~B. -4#This is not f(0).
=C. -7#Correct. f(0)=-7.
~D. 7#The sign is wrong.
}


::ALG2_U05_L07_Q04::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L07_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.3; MLA.A2.FN.11; MLA.A2.FN.12
Question:
What is the x-intercept of f(x)=x-5?
{
~A. x=-5#The sign is wrong.
~B. x=0#This finds the y-intercept input.
~C. x=1#This does not make f(x)=0.
=D. x=5#Correct. Set x-5=0.
}


::ALG2_U05_L07_Q05::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L07_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.3; MLA.A2.FN.11; MLA.A2.FN.12
Question:
If a table shows f(1)=3 and f(5)=11, what is the average rate of change?
{
=A. 2#Correct. (11-3)/(5-1)=8/4=2.
~B. 4#This is the input change.
~C. 8#This is the output change.
~D. 14#This adds values instead of comparing change.
}


::ALG2_U05_L07_Q06::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L07_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.3; MLA.A2.FN.11; MLA.A2.FN.12
Question:
Which equation finds intersections of f and g?
{
~A. f(x)+g(x)=0#This finds when the sum is zero.
=B. f(x)=g(x)#Correct. Intersections have equal outputs for the same input.
~C. f(0)=g(0) only#This checks only y-intercepts.
~D. f(x)g(x)=1#This is not the general intersection condition.
}


::ALG2_U05_L07_Q07::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L07_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.3; MLA.A2.FN.11; MLA.A2.FN.12
Question:
A height model gives x=-1 and x=8 for time. Which value should be used?
{
~A. x=-1#Negative time is not viable.
~B. x=0#This was not a solution.
=C. x=8#Correct. Positive time is viable.
~D. Both values#The negative value is not viable.
}


::ALG2_U05_L07_Q08::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L07_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.3; MLA.A2.FN.11; MLA.A2.FN.12
Question:
Which representation is usually best for reading intervals of increase from a visual pattern?
{
~A. A formula only#A formula may help, but intervals are often clearest visually.
~B. A single point#One point cannot show an interval.
~C. A verbal title#A title does not show interval behavior.
=D. A graph#Correct. Graphs show increasing and decreasing intervals visually.
}


::ALG2_U05_L07_Q09::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L07_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.3; MLA.A2.FN.11; MLA.A2.FN.12
Question:
If f(x)=2x+1, what is f(4)?
{
=A. 9#Correct. 2(4)+1=9.
~B. 8#This omits +1.
~C. 6#This uses the wrong operation.
~D. 12#This multiplies by 3.
}


::ALG2_U05_L07_Q10::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L07_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.3; MLA.A2.FN.11; MLA.A2.FN.12
Question:
If g(x)=x^2-4, what are the zeros?
{
~A. x=2 only#This misses x=-2.
=B. x=-2 and x=2#Correct. x^2-4=0 gives both the positive and negative square roots.
~C. x=-4 and x=4#These are not zeros.
~D. x=0 and x=4#These do not both satisfy the equation.
}


::ALG2_U05_L07_Q11::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L07_Q11
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.3; MLA.A2.FN.11; MLA.A2.FN.12
Question:
Which feature describes where a graph crosses the y-axis?
{
~A. x-intercept#That is where y=0.
~B. average rate of change#That compares outputs over an interval.
=C. y-intercept#Correct. The y-intercept occurs when x=0.
~D. domain#Domain is the input set.
}


::ALG2_U05_L07_Q12::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L07_Q12
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.3; MLA.A2.FN.11; MLA.A2.FN.12
Question:
For f(x)=x^2 and g(x)=x+2, which is larger at x=3?
{
~A. g(x)#g(3)=5, which is smaller.
~B. They are equal#9 and 5 are not equal.
~C. Neither exists#Both functions are defined.
=D. f(x)#Correct. f(3)=9 and g(3)=5.
}


::ALG2_U05_L07_Q13::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L07_Q13
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.3; MLA.A2.FN.11; MLA.A2.FN.12
Question:
Solve x^2=x.
{
=A. x=0 and x=1#Correct. x(x-1)=0.
~B. x=1 only#This misses x=0.
~C. x=-1 and x=0#The sign is wrong.
~D. x=2#This is not a solution.
}


::ALG2_U05_L07_Q14::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L07_Q14
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.3; MLA.A2.FN.11; MLA.A2.FN.12
Question:
Which solution is not viable for a length measurement?
{
~A. 5#Positive length can be viable.
=B. -3#Correct. Length cannot be negative.
~C. 0#Zero may be possible depending on context.
~D. 12#Positive length can be viable.
}


::ALG2_U05_L07_Q15::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L07_Q15
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.3; MLA.A2.FN.11; MLA.A2.FN.12
Question:
If f(2)=7 and g(2)=7, what is true?
{
~A. The functions are equal for every input#One matching input does not prove that.
~B. Both functions have slope 7#Function value is not slope.
=C. The graphs intersect when x=2#Correct. Equal outputs at the same input indicate an intersection.
~D. The y-intercepts are 2#This does not follow.
}


::ALG2_U05_L07_Q16::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L07_Q16
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.3; MLA.A2.FN.11; MLA.A2.FN.12
Question:
What does f(x)>g(x) mean on a graph?
{
~A. f is below g#Greater output means f is above g.
~B. f and g are equal#That would be f(x)=g(x).
~C. f has no values#This is not about domain.
=D. f is above g#Correct. f has larger y-values than g for those inputs.
}


::ALG2_U05_L07_Q17::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L07_Q17
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.3; MLA.A2.FN.11; MLA.A2.FN.12
Question:
If a graph crosses the x-axis at -2 and 4, what are the zeros?
{
=A. -2 and 4#Correct. Zeros are x-values where y=0.
~B. 2 and -4#The signs are reversed.
~C. 0 and 4#This misses -2.
~D. -2 only#This misses 4.
}


::ALG2_U05_L07_Q18::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L07_Q18
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.3; MLA.A2.FN.11; MLA.A2.FN.12
Question:
Which calculation gives average rate of change from x=a to x=b?
{
~A. f(b)-f(a)#This is only output change.
=B. (f(b)-f(a))/(b-a)#Correct. Divide output change by input change.
~C. (b-a)/(f(b)-f(a))#This reverses the ratio.
~D. f(a)+f(b)#This adds outputs.
}


::ALG2_U05_L07_Q19::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L07_Q19
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.3; MLA.A2.FN.11; MLA.A2.FN.12
Question:
If f(x)=2x and g(x)=x^2, what is f(3)-g(3)?
{
~A. 3#This reverses the subtraction.
~B. 15#This adds the values.
=C. -3#Correct. 6-9=-3.
~D. 6#This is only f(3).
}


::ALG2_U05_L07_Q20::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L07_Q20
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.3; MLA.A2.FN.11; MLA.A2.FN.12
Question:
Which input gives f(x)=10 for f(x)=2x+4?
{
~A. x=2#This gives 8.
~B. x=4#This gives 12.
~C. x=5#This gives 14.
=D. x=3#Correct. 2(3)+4=10.
}

